@startuml merge-directionality
title MERGE Directionality — Proven Directly Against Neo4j Before Writing Code

skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam sequenceParticipantBorderColor #888888
skinparam sequenceLifeLineBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam sequenceGroupBorderColor #CCCCCC
skinparam sequenceGroupBodyBackgroundColor #FAFAFA

participant "cypher-shell" as C #DBEAFE
participant "Neo4j" as N #FEF9C3

== Same direction twice -- idempotent (the normal repeat-confirmation case) ==

C -> N : MERGE (a:Entity{id:"A"})-[r:PROXIMITY_EVENT{key:K}]->(b:Entity{id:"B"})\nON CREATE SET r.min_distance_metres = 800
N --> C : created, 1 edge

C -> N : MERGE (a:Entity{id:"A"})-[r:PROXIMITY_EVENT{key:K}]->(b:Entity{id:"B"})\nON MATCH SET r.last_seen_ms = ..., r.min_distance_metres = CASE...
N --> C : matched existing edge, still 1 edge

== Reversed direction, same key -- hard error, not a silent duplicate ==

C -> N : MERGE (b:Entity{id:"B"})-[r:PROXIMITY_EVENT{key:K}]->(a:Entity{id:"A"})
N --> C : ERROR -- uniqueness constraint\nviolation on key K
note right of N
  MERGE's pattern match is directional.
  It does not find the edge created in the
  OPPOSITE direction with the same key --
  it tries to CREATE a new one, and the
  constraint on key rejects it outright.
  Edge count stays at 1; nothing corrupts.
end note

== KNOWN_ASSOCIATE existence read -- undirected, no such hazard ==

C -> N : MATCH (a:Entity{id:"A"})-[k:KNOWN_ASSOCIATE]-(b:Entity{id:"B"})\nRETURN k
N --> C : found -- regardless of which\ndirection it was created in

note over C, N
  Direction only matters for MERGE's
  create-or-match write. A plain existence
  read has no such pattern-direction
  constraint -- this is why canonicalPairKey's
  ordering discipline is required before every
  PROXIMITY_EVENT write, but not before a
  KNOWN_ASSOCIATE read.
end note

@enduml
